\p{These are the accompanying mathematical notes for the research
project FNIP. FNIP is read “F-nip”, also a coined acronym for
“Formulating Neuron Interaction Process”: the neuron, its synapses and
the brain studied as dynamical processes in which a brief interaction, a
small nip, is enough to tip the system across a threshold into a new
state, hence the choice of “nip” as a metaphor.}

\p{The notes study the nonlinear dynamics of neurons, synapses and the
brain through dynamical systems, and the higher function and emergent
behavior they give rise to. They also study this behavior through the
lens of control theory, looking at how the brain acts as a controller
and how it may be steered by stimulation and neural interfaces. They
then take a deeper dive into how a neuron is built and develops from a
general cell into circuits capable of self-sustained activity, both
reactive and proactive.}

